; xhci98-amd64.inf - xHCI USB host controller miniport, the 64-bit package
;
; This is the SECOND INF, and it is deliberately a separate file rather than a
; third install path inside src\xhci98.inf. The decision is design record 11
; section 12 decision 2, taken 2026-09-09: merging would have meant widening
; [Manufacturer] from "%Mfg%=XhciModels" to "%Mfg%=XhciModels,NTx86,NTamd64",
; and that single line is the one Windows 98's 16-bit engine parses to find its
; models section. Whether that engine reads only the first field is exactly the
; sort of thing this tree refuses to assume about a file it documents as a
; silent gate in both directions, and assuming it wrongly costs a full re-run
; of all four existing install legs - 98, ME, 2000, XP32 - to prove nothing
; broke. A separate package costs a second INF to keep in sync and no
; re-validation at all. src\xhci98.inf is therefore untouched by this file's
; existence, and Windows 98's engine is never asked the question.
;
; What it installs is the amd64 xhci98.sys, built by
; "scripts\build-driver.cmd <flavor> -amd64" into src\obj<flavour>\amd64\, on
; the NT 5.2 amd64 target: Windows XP Professional x64 and Windows Server 2003
; x64, which are one target and not two (both are NT 5.2.3790, and WDK 7.1
; ships lib\wxp\i386 with no amd64 counterpart, so WNET is the only route to a
; 64-bit driver for them). Task 21.7 read the same six ABI measurements on
; Vista x64 and Windows 7 x64 and all six pass there too, so nothing in this
; file narrows to NT 5.2 - but those two enforce kernel-mode code signing and
; are a separate leg with guests of their own.
;
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; Why every section here is decorated, and what a wrong pick costs
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
;
; A wrong pick fails cleanly in BOTH directions, which is what makes the four
; flat payload directories (release-x86, debug-x86, release-x64, debug-x64)
; safe rather than a trap:
;
;   - a 32-bit engine reading THIS file finds no undecorated models section and
;     no .NTx86 one. Windows 98's engine takes [Manufacturer]'s first field,
;     looks for [XhciModels], and finds nothing to install; Windows 2000 and
;     32-bit XP skip a %Mfg% line whose TargetOSVersion is NTamd64 outright.
;     Either way the answer is "this INF offers no driver", not "here is a
;     64-bit binary for your 32-bit machine".
;   - a 64-bit engine reading src\xhci98.inf finds an undecorated models
;     section, which it ignores outright, and a .NTx86 one, which is not its
;     architecture.
;
; So there is deliberately NO undecorated [Xhci.Dev] and NO [DefaultInstall]
; in this file. Either would be a section a 32-bit engine falls back to - and
; setupapi's decorated-section lookup does fall back - which would copy an
; amd64 xhci98.sys onto a 32-bit machine and create a service pointing at it.
; scripts\inf-gate\check-inf.ps1 -Arch amd64 refuses both by name (PATH-NO9X,
; OS-DEFAULT); they are not omissions to be tidied up later.
;
; The install sections carry .NTamd64 rather than the plain .NT that
; Microsoft's own tools\winxp64-extracted\usbport.inf uses. That file is one
; INF for two architectures and shares its install sections between them; this
; one is single-architecture, so the more specific decoration says what it
; means and leaves no section any other engine can reach.
;
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; The files the OS supplies, and where they come from on this target
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
;
; The same four files as the 32-bit NT path, by the same LayoutFile route,
; with the same flag 16 (COPYFLG_NO_OVERWRITE), and for the same reasons -
; src\xhci98.inf states them at length and they are not restated here. What IS
; specific to this target, read statically on 2026-09-09 from the owner's
; "Win XP SP2 VL x64" media (7-Zip on the ISO and on its two cabinets; nothing
; executed), is which disk and which cabinet each row resolves to. AMD64\
; LAYOUT.INF, with [SourceDisksNames.amd64] giving disk 1 = \amd64 on the base
; CD and disk 100 = \amd64 on the service-pack source:
;
;   usbport.sys = 100,,212480,,,,4_,4,1,3,,1,4    AMD64\SP2.CAB    212,480 B
;   usbhub.sys  = 100,,102400,,,,4_,4,1,3,,1,4    AMD64\SP2.CAB    102,400 B
;   usbd.sys    =   1,,222222,,,,4_,4,1,3,,1,4    AMD64\DRIVER.CAB   7,552 B
;   usbui.dll   =   1,,222222,,,,,2,1,3           AMD64\DRIVER.CAB 123,392 B
;
; The three sizes that can be cross-checked match tools\winxp64-extracted\
; exactly, which is what authenticates the reading. The last three fields are
; the text-mode Setup disposition: 4,1,3 and 2,1,3 both mean "do not copy at
; Setup", so an xHCI-only install has none of the four on disk - measured, not
; assumed, on the task 21.5 guest: usbport.sys, usbhub.sys, usbehci.sys and
; usbd.sys are absent from vm\winxp64.img including dllcache (2026-09-08), and
; usbui.dll is absent from it too (2026-09-09). Both cabinets sit in
; WINDOWS\Driver Cache\amd64 on every install, so the engine resolves a disk-1
; row and disk-100 rows in one pass with no CD, which is the arrangement
; Windows 2000 was already read doing on 2026-09-07.
;
; Two differences from 32-bit XP, both of which cost nothing here but are
; worth having written down: usbd.sys comes from DRIVER.CAB rather than the
; service-pack cabinet, and on 32-bit XP it was on disk already as a
; 4,736-byte stub where on x64 it is not on disk at all.
;
; usbhub20.sys is on no path, as on every other target: XP x64's LAYOUT.INF
; has no row for it at all, so a row here would have no source.
;
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; What this file must keep in sync with src\xhci98.inf
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
;
; The accepted cost of decision 2 is that two files now carry one package's
; facts. That cost is mechanised rather than left to care:
; scripts\inf-gate\test-inf-checks.ps1 compares the two INFs directly and
; fails if they disagree about the hardware ID, the service name, the service
; install's five values, the driver binary's name, the per-device registry
; values and their defaults, the machine-wide selective-suspend value, the
; OS-supplied file list and its flags, or any [Strings] token they share.
; DriverVer is tied to src\xhci_version.h from both files by the gate itself.
; If a change to one of those belongs in one file only, the check is what will
; make you say so out loud.

[Version]
Signature="$CHICAGO$"
Class=USB
ClassGUID={36FC9E60-C465-11CF-8056-444553540000}
Provider=%Provider%
LayoutFile=layout.inf
; Must equal src\xhci_version.h, exactly as src\xhci98.inf's line must - the
; two packages are one release and are cut from one version. The INF gate
; refuses either disagreeing with the header.
DriverVer=09/09/2026,1.1.0.0

; The TargetOSVersion field is what makes this file 64-bit-only, and it is the
; whole of the difference from src\xhci98.inf's undecorated line. It is also
; why the models section below is [XhciModels.NTamd64] and not [XhciModels]:
; the engine appends the decoration it matched.
[Manufacturer]
%Mfg%=XhciModels,NTamd64

[XhciModels.NTamd64]
%XhciDesc%=Xhci.Dev,PCI\CC_0C0330

[SourceDisksNames]
1=%DiskName%,xhci98.sys,,

; Only this project's two files, under the same names they have on the 32-bit
; media: the two architectures' binaries share a filename, which is why they
; cannot share a directory and why the payload is four self-contained ones.
[SourceDisksFiles]
xhci98.sys=1
xhci98.inf=1

; Dirid 10 + System32\Drivers for the drivers, and dirid 11 for usbui.dll,
; which is a user-mode property-page DLL and not a driver. On this target
; dirid 11 is the 64-bit system directory, so the amd64 usbui.dll out of
; AMD64\DRIVER.CAB is the build that lands there.
;
; There is no Inf.CopyFiles here. On the 32-bit media the 9x right-click
; Install copies the INF into %17%; the NT engines keep their own oemN.inf
; copy, so copying it as well would leave two INFs claiming PCI\CC_0C0330.
; This file has no 9x path, so the section has no route that wants it.
[DestinationDirs]
DefaultDestDir=10
Xhci.CopyFiles=10,System32\Drivers
Xhci.CopyNT=10,System32\Drivers
Xhci.CopyUI=11

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; NT 5.2 amd64 - the only install path in this file
; ------------------------------------------------------------------

[Xhci.Dev.NTamd64]
AddReg=Xhci.AddReg.NT,Xhci.AddReg.Global
CopyFiles=Xhci.CopyFiles,Xhci.CopyNT,Xhci.CopyUI

[Xhci.Dev.NTamd64.Services]
AddService=xhci98,0x00000002,Xhci.AddService

[Xhci.AddService]
DisplayName=%XhciSvcDesc%
ServiceType=1                       ; SERVICE_KERNEL_DRIVER
StartType=3                         ; SERVICE_DEMAND_START
ErrorControl=1                      ; SERVICE_ERROR_NORMAL
ServiceBinary=%12%\xhci98.sys
LoadOrderGroup=Base

; The two log values, identical to the 32-bit file's NT half in name, type and
; default, and read by the driver through the same usbport service. Both
; default to 0: the safe configuration is the default and the convenience is
; the thing asked for by name. src\xhci98.inf carries the full description of
; what each rung does and why the door ships shut.
[Xhci.AddReg.NT]
HKR,,XhciLogVerbosity,0x00010001,0
HKR,,XhciLogDebugView,0x00010001,0

; The one machine-wide value, and the only one this package writes outside the
; device's own key. Both routes into this target deliver it - the device
; install above and the right-click Install below - for the reason the 32-bit
; file gives: an install that aborts before its registry phase would otherwise
; leave a machine that already had this driver without it. Every usbport build
; this driver has run under reads it, and a halted xHC cannot report a
; hot-plug.
[Xhci.AddReg.Global]
HKLM,System\CurrentControlSet\Services\USB,DisableSelectiveSuspend,0x00010001,1

; No temporary-name field on this row, unlike the 32-bit file's. That third
; field exists for Windows 98's 16-bit engine, which cannot otherwise replace
; a loaded binary, and it costs a cosmetic xhci98.tmp left in System32\Drivers.
; The NT copy queue schedules a replacement of a file in use by itself, so on
; this path the field would buy nothing and leave the same litter. Microsoft's
; own tools\winxp64-extracted\usbport.inf writes its bare rows the same way.
[Xhci.CopyFiles]
xhci98.sys

[Xhci.CopyNT]
usbport.sys,,,16
usbd.sys,,,16
usbhub.sys,,,16

[Xhci.CopyUI]
usbui.dll,,,16

; Right-click Install - pre-stages the files with no device present. Decorated,
; and with no undecorated counterpart, for the reason at the head of this file.
[DefaultInstall.NTamd64]
CopyFiles=Xhci.CopyFiles,Xhci.CopyNT,Xhci.CopyUI
AddReg=Xhci.AddReg.Global

; ASCII only and CRLF throughout, as in src\xhci98.inf. Windows 98's parser is
; not the reason here - it never reads this file - but the gate holds both to
; one encoding rule, and a file that is 7-bit ASCII cannot have two codepages
; disagree about it.
;
; The strings are deliberately the SAME as src\xhci98.inf's, not variants of
; them. Only one of the two packages can ever install on a given machine, so a
; user reading Device Manager, and a bug report quoting it, should say the same
; thing whichever architecture is underneath. The self-tests compare every
; shared token.
[Strings]
Provider="Yeo Kheng Meng"
Mfg="xHCI98 Project"
DiskName="xhci98 xHCI Miniport Disk"
XhciDesc="USB 2.0 eXtensible Host Controller (xhci98)"
XhciSvcDesc="xHCI USB 2.0 Host Controller Miniport (xhci98)"
